  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 AM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast: \n\nThere is not much active weather to report for this forecast cycle \nas a midlevel split flow pattern with a classic \u201chigh over low\u201d \nblocking set up will keep conditions quiet across the Great Lakes \nRegion the next several days. 500mb ridging with its axis over \nnorthern Ontario will support surface high pressure to influence the \nNorthwoods and keep weather mostly sunny and rain-free. H8 \ntemperatures are currently in the single digits and will warm along \nthe dry adiabats this afternoon, keeping highs in the mid-60s for \nmost locations. The only impactful weather to watch for will be \ncontinued chilly overnight lows tonight through the end of the week. \nEfficient radiational cooling overnight with clear skies will lead \nto minimum temperatures in the upper 30s for most areas. Localized \nareas and typically colder locations across the CWA (interior and \nelevated terrain) will produce frost and pockets of temperatures \nfalling into the low 30s. Confidence of frost headlines remains low \nas frost will likely remain patchy/isolated. \n\nLong range ensembles introduce some low end chances of rainfall as \nan embedded shortwave develops over the Central Plains and lifts \nacross the Great Lakes Region next week. Again, chances remain low \nas jet dynamics depict this wave lifting while remaining very \nmoisture starved. Showers will likely dissipate with time as it \napproaches the CWA, but we will continue to monitor rain potential \nas this is the first chance of precipitation after several days.",
